You are an autonomous worker in a bounded factory loop on the Scalar Commons
repository.

TRACK C — TASK: implement OD-4 pool-scaled gaming costs in the experiments/sc-e1
economic simulator, and measure the effect on wash-trader profitability.

################  ABSOLUTE RULE — NON-NEGOTIABLE  ################
NEVER make a check pass by weakening code. In this task specifically:
  - Do NOT tune the baseline downward to manufacture a "decrease".
  - Do NOT delete, skip, or loosen experiments/sc-e1/tests/test_analyze.py.
  - Do NOT make the strict-decrease check trivially true (comparing a number to
    itself, comparing against a constant you chose, wrapping it in try/except,
    or asserting on a value the mechanism does not actually affect).
  - Do NOT stub the mechanisms so they return the baseline.
The result of this experiment is allowed to be NEGATIVE. If a mechanism does not
reduce wash-trader profitability, report that honestly — a truthful negative
result is a successful outcome of this task. Faking a positive one is the single
worst thing you could do here, because a parameter decision will be made on it.
###################################################################

## Scope — simulation only

Touch ONLY experiments/sc-e1/. Do NOT modify pallets/, runtime/, node/, or
tests/. This is a model, not the chain. No storage migrations, no runtime
constants, no extrinsics.

## Background — read these first, in this order

1. docs/rfcs/OD-4-pool-scaled-costs.md — the design RFC. It defines the problem
   and THREE mechanisms. Read §1 (problem), §2 (notation and baseline algebra),
   §3, §4, §5 (the mechanisms), §6 (comparison), §7 (open questions).
2. experiments/sc-e1/SC-E1-PHASE0-V2-RESULTS.md — the current baseline results.
3. experiments/sc-e1/SC-E1-phase0-sim.py — the simulator you are extending. Note
   its header: it documents model assumptions MA'-1..MA'-8. Any assumption you
   add or change must be documented in the same style, in the same place.
4. docs/VERIFIED-CONSTANTS.md — the real constants. Do not invent values.

The core finding you are testing (RFC §1):

  Every anti-gaming guard priced as a flat CMN constant is measured against a
  reward that scales with the emission pool, so each guard has a pool-size
  expiry date. Gaming costs must scale with the rewards they gate.

The three mechanisms to implement (per the RFC, which is authoritative over this
summary):
  - Mechanism A (§3): pool-indexed MinQualifyingVol — the volume floor scales
    with the pool instead of sitting at a flat 50 CMN.
  - Mechanism B (§4): percentage / pool-scaled completion fee — fee_bps rises
    with P, replacing the flat completion fee.
  - Mechanism C (§5): W-weight capped by cumulative fees burned.

## What to build

### Step 1 — tests first

Add tests under experiments/sc-e1/tests/ (pytest; the existing suite lives
there). Write them BEFORE the mechanism code, from the RFC's algebra. Run them,
watch them fail, then implement. Required coverage:

  - For each mechanism: the break-even pool P* the RFC derives in its §x.1
    algebra, asserted against the mechanism's implementation. If your
    implementation disagrees with the RFC's closed form, investigate and
    document which is wrong — do not silently adjust the test to match the code.
  - Baseline reproduction: with all mechanisms disabled, the simulator must
    reproduce the existing v2 results (this is the anchor proving you did not
    move the baseline).
  - Determinism: same seed in, same numbers out.
  - The strict-decrease property below.
  - Plancks/CMN unit handling stays consistent (no float drift into balances
    where the model uses integers).

### Step 2 — mechanisms, independently switchable

Implement A, B, and C so each can be enabled/disabled independently (flags or a
config object), with all-off reproducing today's baseline exactly. Keep the
existing compute_weight transcription intact as the baseline path.

### Step 3 — the archetype profitability table

The simulator must produce a table of per-archetype profitability across the
existing archetypes:

    A-1 honest worker, A-2 passive staker, A-3 sybil farm,
    A-4 wash trader, A-5 oracle colluder

for the baseline and for each mechanism configuration, swept across a range of
per-era pool sizes P that BRACKETS the break-evens in the RFC (~1.12M CMN/era
for wash, ~2.78M CMN/era for sybil) — i.e. the sweep must include pools where
the flat guards are known to have already expired. Print the table to stdout in
a readable form and also write it as a JSON artifact under experiments/sc-e1/.

Include the honest archetypes in the table deliberately: a mechanism that
suppresses wash trading by also crushing A-1 honest-worker profitability is a
bad mechanism, and the table must make that visible.

### Step 4 — the measured claim

Determine, and state in a results markdown file, whether each mechanism makes
A-4 wash-trader profitability strictly decreasing relative to the baseline
across the swept pool range. Report per mechanism. If a mechanism fails to
achieve it, say so plainly and show the numbers. Also report the effect on A-1
and A-3 so the reader can see the collateral cost.

Document every new model assumption in the simulator header, in the existing
MA'-x style, and note anything the model cannot capture.

## THE GATE (the only definition of success)

    python3 -m pytest experiments/sc-e1/tests -q

This must pass, and it must include:
  - the pre-existing test_analyze.py, still passing, unmodified
  - your new mechanism tests
  - a test asserting the archetype profitability table is produced with all five
    archetypes and the swept pool values present
  - a test asserting A-4 wash-trader profitability is STRICTLY DECREASING versus
    the recomputed baseline under the mechanism configuration(s) your results
    file identifies as achieving it

That strict-decrease test must compare a mechanism-on run against a
mechanism-off run computed in the same test from the same seed — not against a
hardcoded number, and not against a value you can tune. If NO mechanism achieves
strict decrease, do not fabricate one: implement the test so it asserts the
honest finding, write up the negative result, and STOP with an explanation. A
correct negative result with a passing honest test suite is a pass; a green
suite that hides the answer is a failure.

Commit in small scoped steps ("sc-e1: add Mechanism B pool-scaled fee"). Do not
push and do not open a PR — the factory handles that.
